  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 PM MDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nThe subtropical ridge is centered over north Texas, and stretches\nwest-east across the continent from southern California, across\nsouthern New Mexico, and into northern Florida. As a result, we've\nseen a significant jump in temperatures both at the surface and\naloft, and suppressed convective activity. \n\nTemperatures look to top out around 101 or 102 at ELP today, and\nshould reach 105 on Friday (tomorrow). Heat Advisories have been\nraised for El Paso and Las Cruces, and most areas where elevations\nare below 4000 feet. \n\nThe upper ridge will recenter itself near the NM/AZ border\ntomorrow, while an inverted trough associated with the remnants of\nTropical Storm Bertha move into northeastern Mexico. Tomorrow\nlooks like mostly a down day convection wise, but the GFS does\nshow a little pocket of cooler air at 500 mb trying to rotate\naround the edge of the ridge. Something to watch, but still think\nwe'll see only isolated convection in the higher mountains.\n\nWhile the inverted trough still looks to stay too far south to\nimpact our weather much, some recycled upper level moisture will\nwork into the area Saturday. Mid level temperatures still look\natrociously warm, -2 to -3C at 500 mb, but increasing low level\nsoutheasterly flow will boost lower level moisture. Models show\nincreasing precip chances Saturday afternoon and evening, \nespecially across the Gila, despite the warmer air aloft. While\nthere is no obvious disturbance to work with, the GFS does show a\nweak PV anomaly in the 335-345K layer drifting through.\n\nFor Sunday, a very weak inverted trough (only noticeable in 200 mb\nstreamlines) will move across central Chihuahua, with a little 50\nknot ESE \"jet streak\" moving over southern NM. Moisture looks\nlimited mostly to areas west of the Rio Grande (and more so west\nof the the Continental Divide).\n\nMoisture will try to crawl back east early next week, but we will\ncontinue to battle drier air coming in aloft from central Texas.\nAll in all, sort of a sink-or-swim, sputtering start to the \nmonsoon.",
